{"question": "A novel type of PET radiotracer is being developed to evaluate patients with Parkinson\u2019s disease. A clinical research study has enrolled 1,200 patients, half of whom have the disease. The scan is found to be positive in 590 of the 600 patients with known PD, and positive in 20 of the 600 patients without PD. What is the test\u2019s overall specificity?", "options": {"A": "580 / (20 + 590)", "B": "580 / (10 + 580)", "C": "590 / (590 + 20)", "D": "590 / (590 + 10)", "E": "580 / (580 + 20)"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Neurology", "Nuclear Medicine", "Clinical Research", "Diagnostic Imaging", "Psychiatry"], "option_domains": ["Radiology", "Neurology"], "question_analyses": {"Neurology": "In this medical scenario, the condition being depicted is the evaluation of a new PET radiotracer for diagnosing Parkinson's disease (PD). The radiotracer is being tested in a clinical research study involving 1,200 patients, with half (600) known to have PD and the other half (600) known not to have PD. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n- **Population**: The study includes 1,200 patients, with 600 having PD and 600 not having PD. - **Test Performance**:\n  - **True Positives (TP)**: 590 out of 600 patients with PD have a positive scan. - **False Positives (FP)**: 20 out of 600 patients without PD have a positive scan. - **True Negatives (TN)**: 580 out of 600 patients without PD have a negative scan. - **False Negatives (FN)**: 10 out of 600 patients with PD have a negative scan. ### Specificity Calculation:\nSpecificity is the proportion of true negatives (TN) among the total number of actual negatives (i.e., the sum of true negatives and false positives)..", "Nuclear Medicine": "In this medical scenario, the condition being depicted is the evaluation of Parkinson's disease (PD) using a novel PET radiotracer. The specificity of a diagnostic test is a crucial measure of its performance, indicating the proportion of true negativ